#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400674 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083325 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f06fdfdc-bba9-5bc6-8e32-722ec21da7d6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Unknown Population Parameter Using a Percentage Points Table"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6938ada6-d242-5a29-b3cc-1005ff1beb10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2.69$ years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400676} {:answer/id #uuid "f4c15084-f362-505f-8950-6df3b2548ace" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2.74$ years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400677} {:answer/id #uuid "e01f491c-202f-5132-a3f5-3f0f2930aa15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2.65$ years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400678} {:answer/id #uuid "79ea6bf2-0abb-5dd3-8132-52b2488943a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2.57$ years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400679} {:answer/id #uuid "7b383bc1-6fb9-59e8-b057-fb6dccdf74a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2.79$ years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400680}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6938ada6-d242-5a29-b3cc-1005ff1beb10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2.69$ years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400676} :answer-field/id #uuid "eb566678-fdf6-552d-aad6-4bf9b9328ba8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400675}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "42e998b4-da64-533d-80e4-6303495d71cd" :question/math-academy-id "q-132852" :question/problem "The lifetime of a lithium-ion battery produced by a particular company is normally distributed with a mean of $\\mu $ years and a standard deviation of $0.5$ years.\nAdditionally, $3%$ of batteries last less than $1.75$ years. Find the mean $\\mu $, rounded to $2$ decimal places.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $p$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.10\\,$ | $\\,0.15\\,$ | $\\,0.20\\,$ | $\\,0.25\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,-2.3263\\,$ | $\\,-1.8808\\,$ | $\\,-1.6449\\,$ | $\\,-1.2816\\,$ | $\\,-1.0364\\,$ | $\\,-0.8416\\,$ | $\\,-0.6745\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent the lifetime of a randomly selected battery. Then we have $X∼N(\\mu ,{0.5}^{2}),$ and we want to find the mean $\\mu .$ \n\nWe know that $P(X<1.75)=0.03.$ Converting $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<1.75) & =0.03 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{1.75-\\mu }{0.5}) & =0.03 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{1.75-\\mu }{0.5}) & =0.03 \\\\ \\frac{1.75-\\mu }{0.5} & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.03).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.03)=-1.8808.\n$$\n\nFinally then, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1.75-\\mu }{0.5} & =-1.8808 \\\\ 1.75-\\mu  & =-0.9404 \\\\ \\mu  & ≈2.69.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507444 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083325 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f06fdfdc-bba9-5bc6-8e32-722ec21da7d6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Unknown Population Parameter Using a Percentage Points Table"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d935e86d-2878-5713-88af-732c2cd4dd43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9.05" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507145}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d935e86d-2878-5713-88af-732c2cd4dd43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9.05" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507145} :answer-field/id #uuid "eb3c213e-a427-5f30-8b6a-3f5985900ded" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507383}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "62a29958-3d43-5575-a73a-6517a1734f8a" :question/math-academy-id "q-324461" :question/problem "The price of a deli sandwich is normally distributed with a mean of $\\mu $ dollars and a standard deviation of $$1.10.$ In addition, in $90%$ of cases the price is between $$0.80\\mu $ and $$1.20\\mu .$ Find the average price, **rounding your answer to two decimal places**.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $p$ | $\\,0.75\\,$ | $\\,0.80\\,$ | $\\,0.85\\,$ | $\\,0.90\\,$ | $\\,0.95\\,$ | $\\,0.97\\,$ | $\\,0.99\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,0.6745\\,$ | $\\,0.8416\\,$ | $\\,1.0364\\,$ | $\\,1.2816\\,$ | $\\,1.6449\\,$ | $\\,1.8808\\,$ | $\\,2.3263\\,$ |\n\n$\\mu ≈$\n{{field-1}}\n$dollars$" :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent a sandwich price. Then we have $X∼N(\\mu ,{1.10}^{2}),$ and we want to find the mean $\\mu .$ \n\nWe know that $P(0.80\\mu <X<1.20\\mu )=0.90.$ First, we convert this to a cumulative probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.80\\mu <X<1.20\\mu ) & =0.90 \\\\ 2P(\\mu <X<1.20\\mu ) & =0.90 \\\\ P(\\mu <X<1.20\\mu ) & =0.45 \\\\ P(X<\\mu )+P(\\mu <X<1.20\\mu ) & =0.5+0.45 \\\\ P(X<1.20\\mu ) & =0.95.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<1.20\\mu ) & =0.95 \\\\ P(Z<\\frac{0.20\\mu }{1.10}) & =0.95 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{0.20\\mu }{1.10}) & =0.95 \\\\ \\frac{0.20\\mu }{1.10} & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.95).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.95)=1.6449.\n$$\n\nFinally then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{0.20\\mu }{1.10} & =1.6449 \\\\ 0.20\\mu  & =1.809\\,39 \\\\ \\mu  & =\\frac{1.809\\,39}{0.20} \\\\  & ≈9.05\\,\\text{dollars}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507489 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083326 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "68479ded-5f29-5fd9-9604-e25bef7d5e32" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Probability When a Population Parameter is Unknown"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ea413de4-f41d-5bd6-8d46-90d7116bd177" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "51.99" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507195}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ea413de4-f41d-5bd6-8d46-90d7116bd177" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "51.99" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507195} :answer-field/id #uuid "abe2d838-2efe-5d60-8dd7-942ce7ac6cc4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507349}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bd63f548-8bbe-5de7-9356-fc14587f7fab" :question/math-academy-id "q-325117" :question/problem "The heights of oak trees in a park are normally distributed with a mean of $\\mu $ feet and a standard deviation of $8$ feet. Additionally, $25%$ of the trees are shorter than $45$ feet. Find the percentage of trees taller than $50$ feet. **Round your answer to two decimal places.**\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $p$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.10\\,$ | $\\,0.20\\,$ | $\\,0.25\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,-2.3263\\,$ | $\\,-1.8808\\,$ | $\\,-1.6449\\,$ | $\\,-1.2816\\,$ | $\\,-0.8416\\,$ | $\\,-0.6745\\,$ |\n\n*In addition, you're given the following table of values for $p=Φ(z).$*\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-0.2\\,$ | $\\,0.4090\\,$ | $\\,0.4052\\,$ | $\\,0.4013\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.1\\,$ | $\\,0.4483\\,$ | $\\,0.4443\\,$ | $\\,0.4404\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.0\\,$ | $\\,0.4880\\,$ | $\\,0.4840\\,$ | $\\,0.4801\\,$ |\n\nApproximately\n{{field-1}}$%$ of the trees are taller than $50$ feet." :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent the height of a randomly selected tree. Then we have $X∼N(\\mu ,{8}^{2}),$ and we want to find $P(X>50).$  \n\nWe know that $P(X<45)=0.25.$ First, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<45) & =0.25 \\\\ P(Z<\\frac{45-\\mu }{8}) & =0.25 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{45-\\mu }{8}) & =0.25 \\\\ \\frac{45-\\mu }{8} & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.25)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.25)=-0.6745.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{45-\\mu }{8} & =-0.6745 \\\\ 45-\\mu  & =-5.396 \\\\ \\mu  & =50.396.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve for $P(50<X)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>50) & =P(Z>\\frac{50-50.396}{8}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z>-0.05) \\\\  & =1-Φ(-0.05) \\\\  & =1-0.4801 \\\\  & ≈51.99%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, approximately $51.99%$ of the trees are taller than $50$ feet."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083974 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083326 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "68479ded-5f29-5fd9-9604-e25bef7d5e32" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Probability When a Population Parameter is Unknown"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f04dd09-9af6-5af5-b2a7-02412d51615e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.7%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082776} {:answer/id #uuid "95a31260-f0cc-565e-b971-d2bd4d135ec4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.9%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082777} {:answer/id #uuid "bf2a8b1e-34b7-5bdb-960e-a7263f101f35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11.2%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082778} {:answer/id #uuid "dd0255ec-0e04-54e7-ae22-0d510b62b4f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.8%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082779} {:answer/id #uuid "6ac1477f-55e1-52bb-abe7-4f577c033faa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.5%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082780}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8f04dd09-9af6-5af5-b2a7-02412d51615e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.7%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082776} :answer-field/id #uuid "9cf9b764-d015-5366-9df6-e11472a54a5d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082775}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9debd84a-b5d9-54c6-84fa-986684bdfc9b" :question/math-academy-id "q-94702" :question/problem "In a factory, cookies contain $15$ grams of sugar on average. However, $10%$ of cookies contain more than $17$ grams of sugar. Assuming that the amount of sugar in each cookie is normally distributed, find the percent of cookies that contain fewer than $12$ grams of sugar, rounded to $1$ decimal place.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution, *\n\n| $p$ | $\\,0.900\\,$ | $\\,0.950\\,$ | $\\,0.975\\,$ | $\\,0.990\\,$ | $\\,0.995\\,$ | $\\,0.999\\,$ | $\\,0.9995\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,1.2816\\,$ | $\\,1.6449\\,$ | $\\,1.9600\\,$ | $\\,2.3263\\,$ | $\\,2.5758\\,$ | $\\,3.0902\\,$ | $\\,3.2905\\,$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n*In addition, you're given the following table of values for $p=Φ(z)$*.\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-1.9\\,$ | $\\,0.0287\\,$ | $\\,0.0281\\,$ | $\\,0.0274\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.8\\,$ | $\\,0.0359\\,$ | $\\,0.0351\\,$ | $\\,0.0344\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.7\\,$ | $\\,0.0446\\,$ | $\\,0.0436\\,$ | $\\,0.0427\\,$ |" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent the number of grams of sugar in a cookie. Then we have $X∼N(15,{\\sigma }^{2}),$ and we want to find $P(X<12).$ But first, we need to find the standard deviation $\\sigma .$ \n\nWe know that $P(X>17)=0.1.$ First, we convert this to a cumulative probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 17) & =1-P(X>17) \\\\  & =1-0.1 \\\\  & =0.9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 17) & =0.9 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{17-15}{\\sigma }) & =0.9 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{2}{\\sigma }) & =0.9 \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.9)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.9)≈1.2816.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sigma } & =1.2816 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sigma } & =0.6408 \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{1}{0.6408} \\\\  & ≈1.5605.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve for $P(X<12)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<12) & =P(X\\le 12) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le \\frac{12-15}{1.5605}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z\\le -1.92) \\\\  & =Φ(-1.92) \\\\  & =0.0274 \\\\  & ≈2.7%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, approximately $2.7%$ of cookies contain less than $12$ grams of sugar."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083975 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083324 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "179e0482-8d67-5e28-af2d-0d3aa7f0b770" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Probability Given a Normal Distribution Model"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8cb42038-d4e2-5cd2-8193-28bac637ba07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082782} {:answer/id #uuid "292516a9-9b55-5825-97e9-ce95b5702014" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.87" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082783} {:answer/id #uuid "3b79c1d9-f7d4-5316-be66-a07d9bb8fe63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.92" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082784} {:answer/id #uuid "698291c0-9ecf-5cf7-aa1b-9afe2e390f0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.08" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082785} {:answer/id #uuid "6aede979-f402-5d68-8ab7-96a5935c49a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.23" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082786}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "698291c0-9ecf-5cf7-aa1b-9afe2e390f0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.08" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082785} :answer-field/id #uuid "d097420f-f8f8-569f-a975-5e227fecbe9b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082781}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3e774eae-3ccc-5f1e-a35b-4ce8e1ddec45" :question/math-academy-id "q-94971" :question/problem "The test scores of a group of students are normally distributed with a mean of $73$ and a standard deviation of $5.$ What is the probability that a randomly selected student got a score of $80$ or more? Round your answer to $2$ decimal places.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,1.3\\,$ | $\\,0.9032\\,$ | $\\,0.9049\\,$ | $\\,0.9066\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.4\\,$ | $\\,0.9192\\,$ | $\\,0.9207\\,$ | $\\,0.9222\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.5\\,$ | $\\,0.9332\\,$ | $\\,0.9345\\,$ | $\\,0.9357\\,$ |" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent the score of a randomly selected student. Then we have $X∼N(73,{5}^{2}).$\n\nWe want to find $P(X\\ge 80).$ Thus, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 80) & =P(Z\\ge \\frac{80-73}{5}) \\\\  & =P(Z\\ge 1.4) \\\\  & =1-P(Z\\le 1.4) \\\\  & =1-Φ(1.4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\n1-Φ(1.40)=1-0.9192≈0.08.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that a randomly selected student got $80$ or more is $0.08.$"}]}
